\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Weighting modes for common-support testing. \textbf  {(a)} Weight as a function of domain probability $p$ for six approaches: unweighted (uniform), Crump trimming (binary mask at threshold $\alpha =0.1$), overlap weights ($p(1-p)$), and stabilized RIW (forward, inverse, doubly weighted). Overlap weights are symmetric---the same function $w(p)$ applies to both groups---and reach zero at the boundaries. Stabilized RIW weights are asymmetric: forward (source) and inverse (target) corrections handle each group's density structure separately and remain bounded away from zero. \textbf  {(b)} Concrete example with two-sided mismatch. Source and target densities overlap partially; weak-overlap regions on both tails carry non-comparable mass. The doubly weighted mode (both corrections active) concentrates effective weight on the common-support region (shaded), downweighting observations from low-overlap tails. The asymmetric corrections are what allow RIW to resolve two-sided contamination where symmetric schemes (overlap, Crump) cannot.}}{3}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:conceptual-weighting}{{2}{3}{Weighting modes for common-support testing. \textbf {(a)} Weight as a function of domain probability $p$ for six approaches: unweighted (uniform), Crump trimming (binary mask at threshold $\alpha =0.1$), overlap weights ($p(1-p)$), and stabilized RIW (forward, inverse, doubly weighted). Overlap weights are symmetric---the same function $w(p)$ applies to both groups---and reach zero at the boundaries. Stabilized RIW weights are asymmetric: forward (source) and inverse (target) corrections handle each group's density structure separately and remain bounded away from zero. \textbf {(b)} Concrete example with two-sided mismatch. Source and target densities overlap partially; weak-overlap regions on both tails carry non-comparable mass. The doubly weighted mode (both corrections active) concentrates effective weight on the common-support region (shaded), downweighting observations from low-overlap tails. The asymmetric corrections are what allow RIW to resolve two-sided contamination where symmetric schemes (overlap, Crump) cannot}{figure.caption.10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:conceptual-weighting@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][3][]3}{}{}{}}
